    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "743d8d78d7be6d8e46ba42cd63cfcecc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1152,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, I think we should start off by addressing why the Twenty Second Amendment to the Constitution Bill is important and why it matters. The discussion on the Twenty Second Amendment to the Constitution Bill underscores the divisive nature of politics in Sri Lanka over the decades. It is truly sad to see this important legislation being framed so narrowly in a classic case of missing the woods for the trees.\n\nIn fact, it is Supreme Court Judge, Justice Kodagoda who recently disclosed that there are more than 1.1 million cases pending in courts and that it takes 10 years and 2 months to conclude a single cycle of a criminal case in this country. This fact was indeed affirmed by the Hon. Minister Ananda Wijepala in his speech today. That cycle is just the first phase of a case. If there is an appeal made, it would take another considerable amount of time and money. Therefore, just consider the enormity of the problem we are facing today with 1.1 million cases pending under the system, Sir. That is why we must urge all the Members of Parliament, legal professionals and our people to consider the total package the Government is unveiling."
  },
  {
