    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/24400_english_2026-09-24.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-24-2026-09-24-0151",
    "chunk_index": 151,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "272539321e77182b011c60e63da122a6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1870,
    "chunk_text": "In fact, it is Supreme Court Judge, Justice Kodagoda who recently disclosed that there are more than 1.1 million cases pending in courts and that it takes 10 years and 2 months to conclude a single cycle of a criminal case in this country. This fact was indeed affirmed by the Hon. Minister Ananda Wijepala in his speech today. That cycle is just the first phase of a case. If there is an appeal made, it would take another considerable amount of time and money. Therefore, just consider the enormity of the problem we are facing today with 1.1 million cases pending under the system, Sir. That is why we must urge all the Members of Parliament, legal professionals and our people to consider the total package the Government is unveiling.\n\nUnder the Twenty Second Amendment to the Constitution Bill and the Judicature (Amendment) Bill, no judge receives a personal extension at the pleasure of the President or any other Member of the Government. There is no application form, no political favours or Executive discretion. The rule is same for everyone; from the Magistrate's Courts to the Supreme Court, it is a general application of one rule. That is precisely why the Supreme Court rejected the argument that the Amendment amounts to a personal inducement. That was addressed in the Special Determination on the Twenty Second Amendment to the Constitution Bill and the Judicature (Amendment) Bill, Sir. The Court said that the proposed Amendment applies to all judges and is not a special benefit requested by or granted to a particular judge. Addressing the issue of conflict of interest, it had been distinguished between the direct personal interest in a specific case and a general institutional interest arising from the legislation, affecting the Judiciary. Therefore, this is not about one judge or one case, this is about all the judges across the spectrum."
  },
