    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/24400_english_2026-09-24.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-24-2026-09-24-0156",
    "chunk_index": 156,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "d5eef68e4f2eb2c844eaa16baf0b6436",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 914,
    "chunk_text": "Also, there is a fundamental difference between reducing judges' tenure and increasing the retirement age uniformly. A reduction threatens the security of tenure. It tells judges that Parliament or the Executive can shorten their service. A reduction definitely threatens the independence of the Judiciary. A general increase does not remove a judge from office or make the continued service dependent on pleasing the Government. This is a general extension awarded to all judges. Therefore, Sir, their work in the future is not dependent on pleasing the Government or the Executive. That is the reality of the Twenty Second Amendment.\n\n2109 2110\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0db8\u0dda\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\ncan and should make that change or amendment. However, if we leave it to the Executive, that is an entirely different matter, Sir. Then, that will be discretionary, which the Twenty Second Amendment does not seek to do."
  },
