    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/24400_english_2026-09-24.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-24-2026-09-24-0159",
    "chunk_index": 159,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "a623cb0da8b52615b70d69a9f46c4e2e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2096,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, we should definitely learn from the past, but should not be imprisoned by it, especially when we exercise the sovereign power of the people. The Impeachment of Shirani Bandaranayake showed the danger of political interference; the treatment of a successor showed the danger of changing governments treating judicial office as a political prize. The answer is not to leave the law frozen in place; the answer is to establish clear, general and predictable rules that apply to everyone. No judge should hold office because a politician prefers him. No judge should leave office because a politician dislikes the judgment given by him. No judge should depend on the Executive generosity for continued service. That is the principle behind this Amendment. The Court of Appeal and the High Court are being strengthened for the public. The Office of the Chief Justice is being protected from indefinite concentration of power by introducing a cap, Sir. Therefore, the Government asks Parliament to judge this Amendment not by suspicion or speculation, but by its text, the structure and its effect.\n\nI hope the Members of Parliament voting on this Amendment, the members of the public and especially, the members of the legal community would take into consideration that this is not a favour or a political appointment, but a very clear set of rules established by the Parliament of the country, which is empowered to do so. That is the context of the Twenty Second Amendment to the Constitution and that is how we should think and talk about it, not on the petty political terms it is being framed right now, Sir.\n\nI thank you, Sir, for giving me time to make my speech.\n\n\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0db1\u0dd2\u0dba\u0ddd\u0da2\u0dca\u200d\u0dba\u0dba \u0d9a\u0dae\u0dcf\u0db1\u0dcf\u0dba\u0d9a\u0dad\u0dd4\u0db8\u0dcf\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0baa\u0bbf\u0bb0\u0ba4\u0bbf\u0b9a\u0bcd \u0b9a\u0baa\u0bbe\u0ba8\u0bbe\u0baf\u0b95\u0bb0\u0bcd \u0b85\u0bb5\u0bb0\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bb3\u0bcd) (The Hon. Deputy Speaker) Next, the Hon. Anuradha Jayaratne. You have 11 minutes.\n\n2111 2112\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0db1\u0dd3\u0dad\u0dd2\u0da5 \u0dbd\u0d9a\u0dca\u0db8\u0dcf\u0dbd\u0dd2 \u0dc4\u0dda\u0db8\u0da0\u0db1\u0dca\u0daf\u0dca\u200d\u0dbb\u0dbb \u0db8\u0dd9\u0db1\u0dd9\u0dc0\u0dd2\u0dba] ]\n\n2026 \u0dc3\u0dd0\u0db4\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd0\u0db8\u0dca\u0db6\u0dbb\u0dca 24"
  },
