    "chunk_text": "\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0da2\u0dd3.\u0da2\u0dd3. \u0db4\u0ddc\u0db1\u0dca\u0db1\u0db8\u0dca\u0db6\u0dbd\u0db8\u0dca \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dcf\u0dad\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0b9c\u0bc0.\u0b9c\u0bc0. \u0baa\u0bca\u0ba9\u0bcd\u0ba9\u0bae\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bb2\u0bae\u0bcd) \u0ba9\u0bcd\u0ba9\u0bae\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bb2\u0bae\u0bcd) (The Hon. G.G. Ponnambalam) Hon. Presiding Member, today, we are debating the Twenty Second Amendment to the Constitution Bill and the Judicature (Amendment) Bill. Whilst this Debate was going on, an Hon. Lady Member from the Government, while speaking, quoted Lord Denning. Lord Denning, Sir, was an iconic judge - a Law Lord - because he had a very liberating attitude towards approaching the law and interpreting it. One of the key cornerstone principles he left behind as a legacy was that the law must be alive, it must not be restricted to mere words, must not simply be looked at with a literal meaning and that you must go behind it and understand the concepts on which these laws are created. So, he is someone most law students adored because of his very progressive way of looking at things. The Hon. Lady Member mentioned Lord Denning and said that we must go to the substance of what is being intended and not look at the actual letter of the law. I completely agree with her, and it is in that spirit that I would like to make the first part of my intervention, which is with regard to process, Sir.\n\nThey could have easily cornered the Members of the Opposition by having a consultative process, bringing in the civil society in a big way, but they chose not to do that. They chose not to do that in a background where this Government had never, never, stated the increase in the retirement age of the judges as one of their pledges during"
