    "chunk_size_bytes": 2064,
    "chunk_text": "[3.01 p.m.] \u0d9c\u0dd4 \u0dd4\u0dc0\u0db1\u0dca \u0dc0\u0dd2\u0db4\u0dda\u0dd3\u0dbb \u0dd9\u0dad\u0dad\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0bb0\u0bc1\u0bb5\u0ba9\u0bcd \u0bb5\u0bbf\u0bb5\u0b9c\u0bb5\u0bc0\u0bb0) (The Hon. Ruwan Wijeweera) Thank you very much, Hon. Deputy Chairperson of Committees, for giving me this opportunity to be part of\n\n2343 2344\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0da0\u0db1\u0dca\u0dc4\u0daf\u0db1 \u0dd6 \u0dbb\u0dd2\u0dba\u0d86\u0dbb\u0da0\u0dca\u0da0\u0dd2 \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dcf]\n\n2026 \u0dc3\u0dd0\u0db4\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd0\u0dca \u0db6\u0dbb\u0dca 25\n\nthis important Debate on the Twenty Second Amendment to the Constitution Bill and the Judicature (Amendment) Bill.\n\nSir, I believe everyone knows that we are slowly emerging from the worst economic crisis in our recent history; we are moving from a period of stabilization towards growth. That growth must be equitable, sustainable and founded on strong institutions. It must be supported by a determined effort to tackle bribery, corruption and the abuse of public trust.\n\nMadam, what Parliament is considering today is part of that wider programme of judicial and criminal justice reform. It is intended to strengthen the capacity of the courts, improve administration of justice and provide a more effective foundation for enforcing the law.\n\nThe Supreme Court has now determined that the provisions of the Twenty Second Amendment to the Constitution Bill do not require approval by the people at a Referendum. The Court has also determined that the Judicature (Amendment) Bill, too, is not inconsistent with the Constitution, and may be passed by a simple majority of Parliament. The Court further found that the relevant provisions of the two Bills apply on a non-discriminatory and non-discretionary basis. It concluded that the provisions do not impair the independence of the Judiciary in the manner alleged by the petitioners.\n\nMadam, it clears the Constitutional path for Parliament to consider and pass these reforms with the required majority. It does not remove Parliament\u2019s responsibility to debate the provisions carefully nor does it diminish the importance of judicial independence. Both these requirements have been respected by the Supreme Court."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
