    "chunk_index": 161,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "5dc84fa19e1d0a36179239d05208ca23",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1995,
    "chunk_text": "Madam, it clears the Constitutional path for Parliament to consider and pass these reforms with the required majority. It does not remove Parliament\u2019s responsibility to debate the provisions carefully nor does it diminish the importance of judicial independence. Both these requirements have been respected by the Supreme Court.\n\nMadam, our position, the Government\u2019s position, in this regard is very clear: institutions must be allowed to do their work; investigations must be conducted\n\naccording to the law; prosecutors must exercise their independent responsibilities; courts must decide on cases based on evidence and in accordance with the law and accountability must not depend on political connections. That is the stance and the position of the Government in this regard.\n\nThe Twenty Second Amendment to the Constitution is not only a part of the wider judicial reforms this Government has undertaken: it is not only a part. There is no doubt that the Supreme Court\u2019s Determination gives Parliament the Constitutional guidance necessary to move forward. But, this is not a moment for triumphalism. It is not a contest in which the Government has defeated the Opposition; it is not a victory for one political party over another. So, there is no defeat or victory in this regard. This is a collective effort that we all must strive for. It is a step forward for the people who wait for justice. Madam, we all know that the people of this country have been waiting for a long period of time for justice to be meted out. So, we all should understand that as well.\n\nFor too long, citizens have been entering courts seeking relief and have been facing years of delay. Families wait for decisions; victims wait for a closure of their cases; accused persons wait for their cases to be heard; businesses wait for disputes to be resolved; witnesses grow old; evidence becomes more difficult to be preserved and public confidence suffers whenever justice is delayed beyond a reasonable time"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-25-2026-09-25",
    "num": "2026-09-25",
    "date_str": "2026-09-25",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-09-25",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/24440_english_2026-09-25.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-25-2026-09-25-0162",
    "chunk_index": 162,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "7afc9bf8e5b2c40dc59270cf4229cba9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1738,
    "chunk_text": "For too long, citizens have been entering courts seeking relief and have been facing years of delay. Families wait for decisions; victims wait for a closure of their cases; accused persons wait for their cases to be heard; businesses wait for disputes to be resolved; witnesses grow old; evidence becomes more difficult to be preserved and public confidence suffers whenever justice is delayed beyond a reasonable time\n\nMadam, the Judicature (Amendment) Bill must, therefore, be read together with the wider judicial reform programme. It raises the retirement age of judges by two years on a non-discriminatory and non-discretionary basis. It also provides for an increase in the number of Judges in the Court of Appeal and the High Court, together with safeguards concerning an individual who holds the Office of Chief Justice for an extended period.\n\nThese are not measures designed to favour one individual or one political faction. The common argument the Opposition is levelling against us is nothing else, but that this Constitutional reform is targeting one individual, which is very wrong and false. They are structured as general institutional measures. The Supreme Court considered the relevant provisions and concluded that the two Bills, taken as a whole, do not impinge on the independence of the Judiciary in the manner alleged by the petitioners.\n\n2345 2346\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dd2\u0d9f\u0dca \u0db1\u0dca\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\nJudicial reform cannot be reduced to slogans; it must be assessed against the Constitution, the separation of powers, the independence of judges and the practical ability of courts to deliver justice. The Court has performed its constitutional function and Parliament must now perform its legislative function responsibly."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-25-2026-09-25",
