    "chunk_index": 163,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "b2f76269eb63cd032bb72f037568c3c3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1613,
    "chunk_text": "Judicial reform cannot be reduced to slogans; it must be assessed against the Constitution, the separation of powers, the independence of judges and the practical ability of courts to deliver justice. The Court has performed its constitutional function and Parliament must now perform its legislative function responsibly.\n\nA modern justice system requires a complete chain, from investigation to prosecution, from forensic examination to court administration, from filing of a case to the delivery and enforcement of the judgment. That is why the Government has announced plans to strengthen the entire criminal justice system. Those plans include the recruitment of an additional 10,000 police officers, the expansion of forensic capacity through thousands of additional forensic technicians and recruitment and strengthening of court clerks and other essential personnel.\n\nThe reforms must also be accompanied by accountability. Additional resources must produce measurable results. The public deserves transparent information about vacancies, case disposal, adjournments, forensic turnaround times and the age of pending cases. The Government must be prepared to report progress, identify failures and correct them.\n\nMadam, at the same time, efficiency must never mean abandonment of fairness. There are various things to be done to achieve that target. The rights of accused persons must be protected; victims must be treated with dignity; judges must be able to decide cases independently and without fear and legal representation, due process and the rule of law must remain the foundation of every reform."
  },
