    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/24440_english_2026-09-25.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-25-2026-09-25-0167",
    "chunk_index": 167,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "667cbb71be225d03abab4ce438f20aff",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1716,
    "chunk_text": "Then, we observe that the Supreme Court\u2019s own Determination creates an important distinction concerning the Chief Justice. The Court required that the Bill expressly provide that that six-year term limit will not apply to the person holding the Office of Chief Justice when the Act comes into operation. That is constitutionally significant. Why was that transitional provision necessary? Why would applying the six-year term limit to the incumbent potentially raise a tenure issue? Then, may I ask, if changing the tenure of the incumbent Chief Justice requires protection, why should changing the retirement age of other incumbent judges be treated as institutionally insignificant? Does this not demonstrate that changes affecting the serving judicial office-holders require particular constitutional caution? I would dare say that the suggested Amendment by the Court itself demonstrates the importance of prospective protection of judicial tenure.\n\nThen, Madam, let us examine the argument of institutional conflict of interest. The petitioners\u2019 argument deserves Parliamentary consideration even though the Court rejected it. Their concern, essentially, was that the Bill directly affects the retirement age of the serving judges. The Judges hearing the constitutional challenge themselves are members of the Judiciary, whose institutional conditions would be affected. Therefore, the question arises whether the ordinary principle that no person should adjudicate a matter in which he or she has a direct interest should apply. The Court rejected that argument, citing the doctrine of necessity.\n\nThe doctrine of necessity may answer the question, who can constitutionally hear the case, if no alternative"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-25-2026-09-25",
