    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/24440_english_2026-09-25.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-25-2026-09-25-0168",
    "chunk_index": 168,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "2ea328d424dda6809f12a633615193f0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1726,
    "chunk_text": "Then, Madam, let us examine the argument of institutional conflict of interest. The petitioners\u2019 argument deserves Parliamentary consideration even though the Court rejected it. Their concern, essentially, was that the Bill directly affects the retirement age of the serving judges. The Judges hearing the constitutional challenge themselves are members of the Judiciary, whose institutional conditions would be affected. Therefore, the question arises whether the ordinary principle that no person should adjudicate a matter in which he or she has a direct interest should apply. The Court rejected that argument, citing the doctrine of necessity.\n\nThe doctrine of necessity may answer the question, who can constitutionally hear the case, if no alternative\n\nThen, let me come to Article 108, diminution vs enhancement. The Court\u2019s substantive reasoning distinguishes a reduction in judicial tenure or emoluments from a general enhancement of tenure applicable across the Judiciary. The Court treated the proposed extension as an across-the-board enhancement rather than a reduction or a selective benefit. But, Parliament should consider a further question. The fact that something is an enhancement does not automatically eliminate every institutional concern. For example, an additional two years has economic value, it extends the period during which a judge remains in office, changes the composition of the Judiciary and also potentially changes the timing of succession and affects appointments, which would otherwise become available. Therefore, an enhancement of tenure could still have constitutional and institutional consequences, even if it is not a diminution of existing rights. That is my argument, Madam."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-25-2026-09-25",
