    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/24440_english_2026-09-25.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-25-2026-09-25-0169",
    "chunk_index": 169,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "24d01772eda2da7a33c6e4401b82284b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2153,
    "chunk_text": "Then, let me come to Article 108, diminution vs enhancement. The Court\u2019s substantive reasoning distinguishes a reduction in judicial tenure or emoluments from a general enhancement of tenure applicable across the Judiciary. The Court treated the proposed extension as an across-the-board enhancement rather than a reduction or a selective benefit. But, Parliament should consider a further question. The fact that something is an enhancement does not automatically eliminate every institutional concern. For example, an additional two years has economic value, it extends the period during which a judge remains in office, changes the composition of the Judiciary and also potentially changes the timing of succession and affects appointments, which would otherwise become available. Therefore, an enhancement of tenure could still have constitutional and institutional consequences, even if it is not a diminution of existing rights. That is my argument, Madam.\n\nThen, Madam, let me also deal with the argument on judicial experience. I do not dispute the value of experience. But, experience is an argument for judicial policy; it is not necessarily an argument for changing the tenure of judges already holding office. The two questions should be separated. What should the retirement age be in the future? Should that new age be applied to judges already in office? With regard to judicial vacancies and court delays, the objective is to reduce delays and increase the number of judges.\n\n2349 2350\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dd2\u0d9f\u0dca \u0db1\u0dca\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\n\u0d9c\u0dd4 \u0db1\u0dd2\u0db4\u0dba \u0db1\u0dba \u0d9a\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0d9a \u0dc3\u0db7\u0dcf \u0dc3\u0db7\u0dcf\u0db4\u0dad\u0dd2\u0dd4\u0dbb\u0dd2\u0dba\u0dba (\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0b95\u0bc1\u0bb4\u0bc1\u0b95\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bb3\u0bbf\u0ba9\u0bcd \u0baa\u0bbf\u0bb0\u0ba4\u0bbf\u0ba4\u0bcd \u0ba4\u0bb5\u0bbf\u0b9a\u0bbe\u0bb3\u0bb0\u0bcd \u0b85\u0bb5\u0bb0\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bb3\u0bcd) (The Hon. Deputy Chairperson of Committees) \u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0db8\u0db1\u0dca\u0dc4\u0dd3\u0dad\u0dd4\u0db8\u0dcf, \u0d94\u0db6\u0dad\u0dd4\u0db8\u0dcf\u0da7 \u0d9f\u0dc0\u0db1\u0dca\u0dc4\u0dc0\u0dd6 \u0d9a\u0dcf\u0dbd\u0dba \u0d85\u0dc0\u0dc3\u0dcf\u0db1\u0dba\u0dd2. \u0d9c\u0dd4 \u0db1\u0dd3\u0dad\u0dd2\u0da5 \u0dbb\u0dc0\u0dd4 \u0db1 \u0dad\u0d9a\u0dd3\u0db8\u0dca \u0dd9\u0dad\u0dad\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0b9a\u0b9f\u0bcd\u0b9f\u0ba4\u0bcd\u0ba4\u0bb0\u0ba3\u0bbf \u0bb0\u0b8a\u0baa\u0bcd \u0bb9\u0b95\u0bc0\u0bae\u0bcd) (The Hon. Rauff Hakeem, Attorney-at-Law) Madam, I will conclude."
  },
