    "chunk_size_bytes": 1927,
    "chunk_text": "Furthermore, on the Constitutional Council question, the petitioners argued that extending the tenure of sitting judges effectively changes the period for which judges remain in office without requiring a fresh appointment or fresh constitutional scrutiny.\n\nThe Court also reaffirmed the importance of Article 121(1)\u2019s requirement concerning delivery of petitions to the Hon. Speaker at the same time as filing in Court. The parliamentary lesson of it is that the Constitutional litigation concerning Bills is subject to strict procedural requirements. Parliament should likewise exercise particular procedural care when altering constitutional provisions concerning the judiciary.\n\nWould we want a future Government to have exactly the same power that we are exercising today? Imagine a future Government faces difficult litigation; disagrees with particular judicial decisions; changes the retirement age; thereby alters when vacancies arise; changes the composition and succession of the superior courts. Even if today\u2019s Government acts in complete good faith, the constitutional precedent remains.\n\nI would suggest three possible safeguards. The first is prospective application. Existing judges remain under the present retirement regime. The new retirement age applies to judges appointed after commencement. The second safeguard I would suggest is, comprehensive reform. Instead of a piecemeal amendment, Parliament could examine retirement age, judicial appointments, Chief Justice tenure, number of judges, court administration, case backlog, regional sittings and judicial resources. The third one is transitional safeguards. If Parliament proceeds with the Amendment, it should clearly define the treatment of every serving judge, avoid selective or personalized provisions, establish objective transitional rules and ensure that no future Government can manipulate tenure for short-term institutional advantage."
  },
