    "chunk_size_bytes": 1153,
    "chunk_text": "I would suggest three possible safeguards. The first is prospective application. Existing judges remain under the present retirement regime. The new retirement age applies to judges appointed after commencement. The second safeguard I would suggest is, comprehensive reform. Instead of a piecemeal amendment, Parliament could examine retirement age, judicial appointments, Chief Justice tenure, number of judges, court administration, case backlog, regional sittings and judicial resources. The third one is transitional safeguards. If Parliament proceeds with the Amendment, it should clearly define the treatment of every serving judge, avoid selective or personalized provisions, establish objective transitional rules and ensure that no future Government can manipulate tenure for short-term institutional advantage.\n\nHon. Deputy Chairperson of Committees, I wish to conclude by making it absolutely clear that my position is not directed against any individual judge. I make no allegation against the Chief Justice or against any other member of the judiciary. I have full respect for the dignity, integrity and constitutional office of our judges."
