    "chunk_index": 173,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "004c5948d6d08ec8a39b1a29d3f86e47",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1994,
    "chunk_text": "Hon. Deputy Chairperson of Committees, I wish to conclude by making it absolutely clear that my position is not directed against any individual judge. I make no allegation against the Chief Justice or against any other member of the judiciary. I have full respect for the dignity, integrity and constitutional office of our judges.\n\nI am not suggesting that the present Government has an improper motive. I am saying that Constitutions must be designed not only for the Government we have today, but for every Government that may come after us. Good faith today cannot be the only safeguard against misuse tomorrow. The Supreme Court\u2019s own determination has recognized the importance of protecting the incumbent Chief Justice from the new six-year limitation by requiring an express transitional proviso. That demonstrates that changes affecting judicial tenure require careful consideration of the position of serving office-holders.\n\nTherefore, my submission to this House is simple. Let us not personalize this issue. Let us not question the integrity of any judge. Let us not question the constitutional authority of Parliament, but let us protect the institution. If we believe that judicial retirement ages should be increased, let us establish that rule prospectively. If we believe broader judicial reform is necessary, let us undertake that reform comprehensively and transparently. The Constitution should not be written for the judges who happen to occupy the Bench today, nor for the Government that happens to occupy these Benches today. It should be written for the judges, Governments and Parliaments of tomorrow. That is the constitutional responsibility before us today. The contemporaneous report confirms that counsel requested a Full Bench and that the request was rejected, while the Government response relied on Article 132(3); it does not support saying that a Bench larger than five was constitutionally impossible.\n\n2351 2352\n\n2026 \u0dc3\u0dd0\u0db4\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd0\u0dca \u0db6\u0dbb\u0dca 25"
  },
  {
