    "chunk_size_bytes": 1368,
    "chunk_text": "OFFICIAL REPORT THE DEMOCRATIC SOCIALIST REPUBLIC OF SRI LANKA\n\nTENTH PARLIAMENT - FIRST SESSION\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0daf\u0dda\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dd9\n\n02nd of October, 2026, which had been submitted to me by the Hon. Ajith P. Perera, Member of Parliament for the Electoral District of Kalutara.\n\nThe basis of his representation is that no formal action or final decision has been communicated regarding a written request dated 03rd of August, 2026 signed by 18 Opposition Members, seeking the appointment of a Special Select Committee under Standing Order Nos. 100 to 110 to investigate and report on the delays of the judicial system and prison overcrowding.\n\nThe Hon. Member requests that the matter be referred to the Committee on Ethics and Privileges for investigation and recommendation.\n\nI have carefully considered the submission, the relevant provisions of the Parliament (Powers and Privileges) Act, our Standing Orders and the long-established parliamentary practice and precedents, both in Sri Lanka and in the House of Commons of the United Kingdom.\n\nI deliver my Ruling on the admissibility of this application as follows:\n\nOn the immunity and position of the Chair\n\nUnder the constitutional architecture of Parliament, the Speaker is the chief officer, the presiding authority and the guardian of the powers, rights and Privileges of the House as a collective body."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-10-06-2026-10-06",
    "num": "2026-10-06",
    "date_str": "2026-10-06",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-10-06",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/24450_english_2026-10-06.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-10-06-2026-10-06-0122",
    "chunk_index": 122,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "534bd0894a1767959defd37f905ebba3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1866,
    "chunk_text": "Under the constitutional architecture of Parliament, the Speaker is the chief officer, the presiding authority and the guardian of the powers, rights and Privileges of the House as a collective body.\n\nGuidance from Erskine May and House of Commons Practice\n\nOur parliamentary practice relies heavily on the established traditions of the House of Commons. Sir\n\nThomas Erskine May, in his authoritative treatise \"Parliamentary Practice\" (25th Edition, Chapter 18), clearly delineates the strict procedural boundaries regarding the Chair:\n\n\u201cThe action of the Speaker cannot be criticized incidentally in debate or upon a point of order\u2026 The conduct of the Speaker cannot be questioned except on a substantive motion, of which notice has been given.\u201d\n\nErskine May further establishes that any attempt to raise a complaint against the Speaker under the guise of a point of Order or a breach of Privilege is out of order. The Chair is exempt from casual challenge or committee investigation for actions taken in an official capacity, precisely to preserve the dignity, independence and authority of the presiding office.\n\nSri Lankan Parliamentary Practice and Precedents\n\nThis position is well-anchored in the jurisprudence of the Parliament of Sri Lanka. Successive Speakers - most notably Speaker Anura Bandaranaike in his historic Rulings on parliamentary jurisdiction - have consistently reaffirmed that the decisions, directions or administrative conduct of the Speaker cannot be challenged by way of Privilege notices or points of Order.\n\nFurthermore: 1. Section 3 of the Privileges Act guarantees freedom of speech, debate and proceedings. The time taken by the Chair or the Parliament to deliberate on an application for a Select Committee does not constitute an infringement on an individual Member\u2019s statutory freedom of speech or parliamentary immunity."
  },
