    "description": "Hansard of 2026-10-06",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/24450_english_2026-10-06.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-10-06-2026-10-06-0221",
    "chunk_index": 221,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "be345f0e499037d0412e8ce1486c9742",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2004,
    "chunk_text": "They had clearly stated that Sri Lanka withstood two or three consecutive shocks, but, unfortunately, our Opposition is not willing to understand that. We came out of an economic crisis; we faced Cyclone \"Ditwah\"; we face a global economic crisis, triggering a fuel crisis and we were also threatened by the tariff changes of the USA. So, that is what the world has understood about this country, and they are also very confident that we could manage this debt, we could repay it and our foreign exchange reserves are good enough for us to move forward.\n\nAt the same time, we have strengthened our fiscal discipline and have consolidated our revenues, which these experts could not do; the experts who could speak in English could not do so for 76 years. That is the reality in this country. They could not consolidate the revenue from 2015 to 2019; they could not manage their expenses and there was never a time or a Budget, through which they could achieve the revenue target. There was never a Budget through which they could stay within the cost they planned in the first year. And, they are the people who governed this country with overspending and inefficiency in revenue collection. Now, they say that we got a higher revenue because of vehicle imports, and that same person stated in this Parliament last year that that is a tactic at which the Government is going to fail and that it is not going to get much revenue from vehicle imports. Now, the tables have turned and they are saying that that is the only reason why we have revenue consolidation. Obviously, not. We climbed from 13.5 per cent to 16.7\n\n93 94\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0daf\u0dda\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dd9\n\nper cent, out of which the additional increase in revenue from vehicle imports was about 1.2 per cent. That is going to continue into this year is what the statistics are showing because people are still importing vehicles. That is what the Fiscal Policy Department has been doing to succeed in all this over the last two-and-a-half years."
  },
