    "chunk_index": 305,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "7bd6fcf53b130107115f41bace721d4c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1609,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, today, the World Bank reports that Sri Lanka's economy has reached pre-crisis levels, but the recovery remains incomplete and uneven with household incomes and labour market outcomes still lagging. Then, the IMF, which issued their Article IV Report just a couple of days ago, states that the economy is fragile, and shifting from stabilization to economic transformation is a necessity, where structural reforms are imperative and absolutely essential. They further state that the Budget must be in line with their programme in order to get the seventh tranche and that further reforms are needed to improve debt collection, maintain tax fairness, for reserve obstacles to be rectified in public institutions and in reconstruction, for the anti-corruption law to be made more public, to improve trade and labour regulations and maintain inflation.\n\nThen, Sir, I would like to refer to the contradictory economic stand that is being taken by the IMF. I believe that once a country goes into bankruptcy, it is necessary to use the IMF to come out of it, but certainly it does not bestow the right on the IMF to push people beyond the accepted, reasonable limit. The people of this country must be saluted for bearing the brunt of the entire economic crisis of our country. They are the ones who helped us get revenue into this country, going through the difficult period. But, even after all that, when there are only three months more starting by the end of the year to go on with the IMF, we still find them putting conditionalities instead of being part and parcel of the success story of this country."
  },
