    "chunk_text": "In selecting Search and Rescue units, a number of factors are taken into consideration, including the location of the incident, distance from the coastline, time elapsed between the actual occurrence of the incident and the time it was reported, condition of the vessel/persons in distress, the prevailing weather and sea conditions, location and response time of available deployable resources, nature of the search area and other relevant factors.\n\nSri Lanka Air Force\n\n1. In accordance with the coordination\n\nmechanism existing between the Sri Lanka Air Force and the Sri Lanka Navy in relation to search and rescue operations at sea, joint search and rescue operations are conducted by deploying air and maritime assets as required. This process is coordinated through the Operations Rooms (Ops Rooms) and the respective command authorities.\n\n2. Upon receipt of information regarding a\n\nvessel or person being in distress at sea, the relevant information is properly coordinated through the Main Operations Room, and the relevant parties are duly informed. Accordingly, rescue operations are conducted using naval and air assets.\n\n3. The Sri Lanka Air Force conducts the\n\nrelevant aerial reconnaissance/surveillance operations as required and carries out aerial rescue operations accordingly.\n\n(ii) A total of 29 Search and Rescue Operations\n\nwere conducted during 2025, while 26 Search and Rescue Operations were conducted during 2026.\n\n(iii) Sri Lanka Navy\n\nAir Force assistance in 2025 - 06 operations\n\nAir Force assistance in 2026 - 08 operations\n\nSri Lanka Air Force\n\n2025 - 20 operations\n\n2026 - 12 operations\n\n(iv) Sri Lanka Navy"
