    "chunk_size_bytes": 1986,
    "chunk_text": "\u2022 Tourist arrivals reached 2,362,521 in 2025, with earnings of US Dollars 3.2 billion. The Current Account recorded a surplus of 1.6 per cent of GDP in 2025, the third consecutive year of surplus, marking a structural improvement in Sri Lanka\u2019s external position.\n\nThe growing trend continues in 2026 and from January to August, 2026, it amounted to US Dollars 6,131 million.\n\n\u2022 Export earnings also grew by 6.3 per cent to US Dollars 13,581.4 million in 2025, whereas the total export earnings from January to August, 2026 had been US Dollars 14,076 million.\n\nFiscal Outcomes\n\n\u2022 The Central Government primary balance improved from a deficit of 3.7 per cent of GDP in 2022 to a historic surplus of 5.4 per cent of GDP in 2025, the highest ever recorded. As at end-June, 2026, the primary balance surplus stood at Rs. 1,244 billion.\n\n\u2022 Tax revenue nearly doubled from 7.3 per cent of GDP in 2022 to 15.4 per cent in 2025. As at end-September, 2026, tax revenue increased significantly compared to the same period of 2025, by recording 85 per cent of the annual target of 2026.\n\n479 480\n\n\u0db1\u0daf\u0d9a\u0dd2\u0dd9 \u0dca \u0dad\u0dd4\u0dcf\n\n\u2022 Central Government debt has also declined from 114.2 per cent of GDP in 2022 to 94.9 per cent in 2025, which is already below the programme target of 95 per cent by 2032.\n\nSocial Outcomes\n\n\u2022 Social spending targets were met for the first time in the programme\u2019s history in 2025.\n\n\u2022 The Government incurred 3.4 per cent of GDP on household subsidies and transfers in 2025, of which 0.7 per cent was directed to the Aswesuma programme.\n\n\u2022 Headline inflation fell from nearly 70 per cent in September, 2022 to 2.1 per cent in December, 2025.\n\nAll in all, the achievements confirm that the EFF programme has delivered transformative outcomes, moving Sri Lanka from crisis to recovery to stabilization within three years, while building an institutional resilience to manage further shocks, including Cyclone \u201cDitwah\u201d and the Middle-East conflict."
