    "md5": "4117fd4b9058ff390a3e33f7f845ed30",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1806,
    "chunk_text": "All in all, the achievements confirm that the EFF programme has delivered transformative outcomes, moving Sri Lanka from crisis to recovery to stabilization within three years, while building an institutional resilience to manage further shocks, including Cyclone \u201cDitwah\u201d and the Middle-East conflict.\n\nPillar 1- Revenue-Based Fiscal Consolidation: Exceeded\n\nThe Central Government primary balance moved from a deficit of 3.7 per cent to 5.4 per cent of GDP in 2025, far exceeding the programme target of 2.3 per cent of GDP. Tax revenue also rose from 7.3 per cent to 15.4 per cent of GDP, nearly doubling within three years. The Public Financial Management Act No. 44 of 2024 was enacted, establishing binding fiscal rules and accountability mechanisms. The Integrated Treasury Management Information System - ITMIS - was fully rolled out, the SVAT system was repealed and replaced with a risk-based VAT refund scheme, and the Fiscal Strategy Statement and the Fiscal Risk Statement were published for the first time in June, 2025. Cost-recovery pricing for fuel and electricity was established through automatic, formula-based adjustment mechanisms.\n\nPillar 2 - Stronger Social Safety Nets: Achieved\n\nThe \u201cAswesuma\u201d social protection programme was established with a new, objectively determined eligibility framework based on a national Social Registry. Social spending targets were met for the first time in the programme\u2019s history in 2025. Energy subsidies were targeted exclusively to low-consumption households, ensuring that support reached those who need it most. The Government incurred 3.4 per cent of GDP on household subsidies and transfers in 2025, of which 0.7 per cent was directed to the \u201cAswesuma\u201d programme.\n\nPillar 3 - Sovereign Debt Restructuring: Substantially Realized"
