    "md5": "5cf83d8b38eeb61a2ebde2a511867249",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1996,
    "chunk_text": "The \u201cAswesuma\u201d social protection programme was established with a new, objectively determined eligibility framework based on a national Social Registry. Social spending targets were met for the first time in the programme\u2019s history in 2025. Energy subsidies were targeted exclusively to low-consumption households, ensuring that support reached those who need it most. The Government incurred 3.4 per cent of GDP on household subsidies and transfers in 2025, of which 0.7 per cent was directed to the \u201cAswesuma\u201d programme.\n\nPillar 3 - Sovereign Debt Restructuring: Substantially Realized\n\nThe Public Debt Management Office was established in December, 2024 and the Medium-Term Debt Management Strategy 2025-2029 was published. Central Government Debt declined from 114.2 per cent of GDP in 2022 to 94.9 per cent in 2025.\n\nPillar 4 - Resorting Price Stability and Rebuilding International Reserves: Achieved\n\nHeadline inflation fell from nearly 70 per cent in September, 2022 to 2.1 per cent in December, 2025. Even amid the Middle Eastern crisis, inflation has remained broadly around the target, recording 6.8 per cent in June, 2026. Gross Official Reserves were rebuilt from near-zero in early 2022 to US Dollars 6.9 billion by end-August, 2026. The Central Bank Act was enacted in July, 2023, establishing price stability as the primary mandate and prohibiting monetary financing. The exchange rate operates under a fully market-determined, flexible framework. Monetary financing has been fully eliminated.\n\nPillar 5 - Safeguarding Financial Stability: Achieved\n\nThe Banking Act was amended in April, 2024, strengthening supervisory standards, governance and audit independence for State-owned banks. The parate execution moratorium was lifted in 2025, enabling banks to resolve Non-Performing Loans and expand credit. Private sector credit was expanded by approximately 25 per cent year-on-year by end-2025.\n\nPillar 6 - Addressing Governance and Corruption Vulnerabilities: Achieved"
  },
