    "chunk_index": 100,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "2dbf099807532390f295047b6a2b9f02",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1833,
    "chunk_text": "(c) The Government acknowledges the challenges and hardships faced by the people of Sri Lanka due to a nation-driven to bankruptcy and the elevated cost of living during the adjustment period. These measures were necessary to restore fiscal sustainability and were not without cost. However, had these reforms not been implemented, the economic situation would have been far worse. The country is now seeing the benefits of these reforms with the benefits of macroeconomic stability and renewed growth being felt directly by the people of Sri Lanka.\n\n1. The Aswesuma Social Protection Programme has ensured that the most vulnerable households were protected throughout the period of adjustment. The Government incurred 3.4 per cent of GDP on household subsidies and transfers in 2025.\n\n2. The labour force participation rate has also\n\nrisen to 49.4 per cent in 2025, reflecting improved confidence and economic opportunities.\n\n3. Energy subsidies-2026 were targeted exclusively to low-consumption households consuming less than 180 kWh per month, ensuring that support was directed to those who need it most rather than subsidizing higher-income households, as the previous system had done.\n\n4. Headline inflation, which peaked as nearly\n\n70 per cent in September, 2022, was brought down to 2.1 per cent in December, 2025. Even in the midst of the Middle Eastern crisis, inflation has remained broadly around the target, recording 6.8 per cent in June, 2026, and is expecting to remain around the flexible inflation target of 5.0 per cent in the medium term. 5. Private sector credit, which contracted\n\nduring the crisis, was expanded by approximately 25 per cent year-on-year by end-2025. 6. Commercial bank lending rates, which reached around 29 per cent in late 2022, have declined to approximately 10.6 per cent by end-July 2026."
  },
