    "chunk_text": "during the crisis, was expanded by approximately 25 per cent year-on-year by end-2025. 6. Commercial bank lending rates, which reached around 29 per cent in late 2022, have declined to approximately 10.6 per cent by end-July 2026.\n\nEconomic Growth: Achieved and Exceeded\n\n\u2022 The recovery has been broad-based. The industry sector was expanded by 7.8 per cent in 2025, supported by construction, manufacturing and mining and quarrying. The service sector, which accounts for 54. 6 per cent of GDP, grew by 3.3 per cent, driven by food and accommodation, financial services, information technology and transportation.\n\n\u2022 The recovery has demonstrated resilience in the face of significant external shocks, Cyclone \u201cDitwah\u201d in November, 2025 and the Middle-East conflict beginning in February, 2026, without abandoning fiscal discipline or reversing core reforms.\n\nEmployment Generation: Materializing\n\n\u2022 The unemployment rate declined to 3.7 per cent in June, 2026 from 4.4 per cent in 2024, reflecting the absorption of workers into a recovering economy.\n\n\u2022 The labour force participation rate rose to 49.4 per cent in 2025, reflecting growing confidence and economic opportunity.\n\n\u2022 Employment generation has been broad-based, with positive contributions from the industry sector, particularly construction, services and agriculture.\n\nExport Expansion: Positive Performance\n\n\u2022 Merchandize export earnings grew by 6.3 per cent to US Dollars 13, 581. 4 million in 2025, driven by improvements across both industrial and agricultural exports.\n\n483 484\n\n\u0db1\u0daf\u0d9a\u0dd2\u0dd9 \u0dca \u0dad\u0dd4\u0dcf\n\n\u2022 Tourism arrivals reached 2,362,521 in 2025 with earnings of US Dollars 3.2 billion. Workers\u2019 remittances grew by 22. 8 per cent to US Dollars 8,076.2 million in 2025.\n\nInvestment Inflows: Showing Early Recovery"
