    "md5": "80bbfe9ce522326785d1a5c2b01ba7d6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1937,
    "chunk_text": "\u2022 Tourism arrivals reached 2,362,521 in 2025 with earnings of US Dollars 3.2 billion. Workers\u2019 remittances grew by 22. 8 per cent to US Dollars 8,076.2 million in 2025.\n\nInvestment Inflows: Showing Early Recovery\n\n(e) Yes. The Government has assessed the impact of\n\nprogramme reforms on SMEs, self-employed persons, salaried employees and vulnerable groups and has provided targeted relief accordingly. Their economic conditions have materially improved as a result of macroeconomic stabilization, lower interest rates and targeted support measures.\n\nSmall and Medium-Sized Enterprises (SMEs) and Self-Employed Persons\n\n\u2022 The normalization of inflation and the progressive reduction of commercial bank lending rates from around 29 per cent in late 2022 to approximately 10.6 per cent by end-June, 2026 have substantially improved operating conditions for SMEs and self-employed persons, who were among the most severely affected by the crisis.\n\n\u2022 Private sector credit expanded by approximately 25 per cent year-on-year by end-2025, restoring the availability of working capital and investment finance for small businesses.\n\n\u2022 In 2025, over Rs. 11 billion was disbursed to more than 1,500 MSMEs under the SMELOC and SMELOC2 credit facilities, supporting enterprise recovery and expansion.\n\n\u2022 The National Credit Guarantee Institution Limited - NCGIL- established as a public-private partnership, provided guarantees worth Rs. 5.1 billion to 1,234 enterprises, significantly improving access to finance for businesses lacking collateral. Of these, 20 per cent of guarantees were provided to women-led businesses, and 25 per cent were directed to priority sectors including agriculture, IT, exports and tourism.\n\n\u2022 The MSME Re-energizing Programme refinanced Rs. 14.6 billion in loans, providing critical relief to both performing enterprises and distressed businesses recovering from the economic crisis.\n\nSalaried Employees"
