    "chunk_size_bytes": 1499,
    "chunk_text": "\u2022 The MSME Re-energizing Programme refinanced Rs. 14.6 billion in loans, providing critical relief to both performing enterprises and distressed businesses recovering from the economic crisis.\n\nSalaried Employees\n\n\u2022 The Personal Income Tax exemption threshold was increased from Rs. 1,200,000 per annum to Rs. 1,800,000 per annum, providing direct relief to lower and middle-income salaried employees.\n\n\u2022 A phased revision of the pensions of all public servants who retired before January, 2020 was introduced, addressing long-standing pension anomalies and improving the financial well-being of retirees.\n\nVulnerable Groups\n\n\u2022 The \u201cAswesuma\u201d Social Protection programme, established with objectively determined eligibility criteria based on the national Social Registry, provides targeted monthly cash transfers to poor, extremely poor, elderly, disabled and chronically ill persons. Social spending targets were met for the first time in the programme\u2019s history in 2025.\n\n\u2022 The Government incurred 3.4 per cent of GDP on household subsidies and transfers in 2025, of which 0.7 per cent was directed to the \u201cAswesuma\u201d programme, ensuring that the most vulnerable households were protected throughout the period of adjustment.\n\n\u2022 Energy subsidies which were provided in response of the Middle-East conflict were targeted exclusively to low-consumption households consuming less than 180 kWh of electricity per month, ensuring that support is directed to those who need it most."
  },
