    "md5": "0142ef317bf0a993ab8db62588bbddde",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1851,
    "chunk_text": "\u2022 Energy subsidies which were provided in response of the Middle-East conflict were targeted exclusively to low-consumption households consuming less than 180 kWh of electricity per month, ensuring that support is directed to those who need it most.\n\n\u2022 Price stability has improved substantially following the unprecedented inflationary pressures experienced during the 2022\n\n485 486\n\n\u0dd3\u0dd0\u0dc0 \u0dbb(\u0dbb\u0da0\u0daf\u0d9a\u0db1)\u0dbb\u0dad\u0db1\u0dd2\u0dbd\u0dca\u0dbb\u0dca\u200d\u0db1\u0dca \u0dc0\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dc4\u0dc0\u0daf\n\n2026 \u0d94\u0dca\u0dd9\u0dc0 \u0db6\u0d9a 08\n\ncrisis. Supported by prudent monetary policy measures and improved macroeconomic conditions, inflation declined significantly during 2024 and 2025, with headline inflation reaching 2.1 per cent by end-2025. Although inflation increased during the first half of 2026, largely reflecting the pass-through of higher global energy prices and related domestic price adjustments, inflation remained broadly aligned with the Central Bank\u2019s medium-term target, and is under control.\n\n\u2022 The external sector has demonstrated notable resilience during the recovery period, with the Current Account of the balance of payments recording a surplus for the third consecutive year in 2025. Improved foreign exchange inflows strengthened liquidity conditions in the domestic foreign exchange market and supported the continued accumulation of official reserve assets, resulting in Gross Official Reserves increasing significantly to around US Dollars 6.9 billion by end-August, 2026, despite external debt servicing obligations and higher imports mainly on account of energy and vehicle imports.\n\n\u2022 The Central Bank has maintained a flexible and market-based exchange rate policy throughout the recovery period, allowing exchange rate movements to reflect underlying market conditions while ensuring orderly functioning of the domestic foreign exchange market."
  },
