    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "6af468cc1910d296b94fa64284f3de19",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2005,
    "chunk_text": "(a) (i) Sri Lanka currently maintains 63 diplomatic missions overseas consisting of 35 Embassies, 13 High Commissions, 11 Consulates General, two Permanent Missions to the United Nations, one Deputy High Commission and one Representative Office.\n\nSir, today diplomacy cannot be limited to political engagement alone; it must also contribute to the country\u2019s economic development. Therefore, our diplomatic network should be viewed not merely as an administrative presence overseas, but also as an important mechanism for promoting Sri Lanka\u2019s national interest internationally.\n\n(ii) No.\n\n(iii) Does not arise.\n\n(b) (i) The total budget allocation for Sri Lanka\u2019s overseas diplomatic missions for the year 2026 under Expenditure Head 112 of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs is Rs.17.71 billion. The allocation supports the functioning and maintenance of our diplomatic network overseas. We recognize that this represents a considerable commitment to public resources.\n\nConsequently, it is important that our missions operate efficiently and that the country obtains meaningful benefits from their activities.\n\n(ii) Yes. The Ministry conducts assessments of the performance of our overseas missions.\n\n(iii) Performance Reports are obtained from each mission for the first, second and third quarters of the year. Furthermore, in accordance with Public Finance Circular No. 02/2020, the Ministry\u2019s Annual Performance Report is submitted to\n\n647 648\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0db8\u0dda\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\nParliament within 180 days of the conclusion of the relevant financial year. These reporting arrangements provide a mechanism for reviewing the activities and performance of our diplomatic missions.\n\n(iv) According to the Ministry\u2019s assessment, no diplomatic mission has been identified as having a poor performance. Nevertheless, this does not remove the need for continuous monitoring improvements and institutional accountability.\n\n(v) Accordingly, does not arise.\n\n(vi) Accordingly, does not arise."
  },
