    "chunk_size_bytes": 1392,
    "chunk_text": "(iv) According to the Ministry\u2019s assessment, no diplomatic mission has been identified as having a poor performance. Nevertheless, this does not remove the need for continuous monitoring improvements and institutional accountability.\n\n(v) Accordingly, does not arise.\n\n(vi) Accordingly, does not arise.\n\n(ii) 1. Chengdu - China.\n\n\u2022 Chengdu is the Capital of Sichuan Province and an important, economic, industrial, commercial and transportation centre in Western China. \u2022 It provides access to rapidly developing regional markets, creating opportunities for Sri Lankan products such as tea, gems, coconut-based products, spices, handicrafts and other exports. \u2022 The Consulate General was temporarily closed on 30th June, 2016, and the Cabinet Approval for its reopening was granted on 01st of December, 2025.\n\n2. Astana - Kazakhstan\n\n\u2022 Kazakhstan is an important economy in Central Asia with significant natural resources and growing opportunities in trade, investment and tourism. \u2022 Sri Lanka and Kazakhstan established diplomatic relations in 1992, and cooperation has developed in several sectors, including aviation and tourism. \u2022 An Embassy in Astana would provide a more direct institutional mechanism for strengthening these bilateral relationships.\n\n\u2022 Cabinet Approval for the establishment of an Embassy in Astana was granted in January, 2024.\n\n3. Bern - Switzerland."
